=> Learn how to remove an EBS disk from an EC2 and attach the same disk again

Think of EBS like an external hard disk connected to your EC2.

EC2 Server
    │
    │ attached
    ↓
EBS Volume
    │
    ↓
/data-ebs

We will temporarily disconnect that EBS disk and then connect it again.

=> Lab Flow

EBS Volume
    ↓
Mounted on /data-ebs
    ↓
Unmount
    ↓
Detach from EC2
    ↓
Disk disappears from Linux
    ↓
Attach same EBS again
    ↓
Disk appears again
    ↓
Mount it
    ↓
Check data

The important thing we learn is:

Detach does NOT mean delete.

Detach means:

EC2  ✖──── EBS

The EBS volume still exists in AWS.

Later we attach it again:

EC2  ─────→ EBS

And the data should still be there.

=> My Lab Setup

I will use your original EBS:

Volume ID:
vol0ca4e0e03074422c2

Currently:

EBS
 ↓
/dev/nvme2n1
 ↓
/data-ebs

Do NOT touch:

/dev/nvme0n1

because that's your EC2 root disk.

Step 1 — Check the data

First let's make sure /data-ebs is currently mounted.

Run:

df -h /data-ebs

You should see something similar to:

Filesystem       Size  Used Avail Use% Mounted on
/dev/nvme2n1      20G  ...   ...   ... /data-ebs

Now:

ls -l /data-ebs

You should see:

lost+found
test-ebs-file

This proves the EBS disk is currently connected and mounted.

Step 2 — Unmount the EBS

Before AWS can safely detach the volume, Linux should stop using it.

Run:

sudo umount /data-ebs
What does umount mean?

Simple:

mount
  =
connect disk to folder

umount
  =
disconnect disk from folder

After unmounting:

EBS disk
   ↓
/dev/nvme2n1

but

/data-ebs
   ↓
not mounted

Check:

df -h /data-ebs

It should no longer show /dev/nvme2n1 mounted there.

Step 3 — Check lsblk

Run:

lsblk -o NAME,SIZE,FSTYPE,MOUNTPOINTS

You may still see:

nvme2n1   20G   ext4

but the MOUNTPOINTS column will be empty.

This is important:

Unmount does not remove the disk.

It only removes the connection between the filesystem and /data-ebs.

Step 4 — Detach from AWS

Now go to:

AWS Console → EC2 → Elastic Block Store → Volumes

Find:

vol0ca4e0e03074422c2

Right-click → Detach volume

Confirm the detach.

Wait until its state becomes:

Available

=> What happened?

Before:

EC2
 │
 └── vol0ca4e0e03074422c2

After detach:

EC2

        vol0ca4e0e03074422c2
        Available

The EBS still exists.

We did NOT delete it.

Step 5 — Check Linux

Back in Ubuntu:

lsblk

You should no longer see the 20G original EBS disk.

Your root disk and LVM disks will still be there.

This demonstrates:

AWS detach
     ↓
Linux loses the block device

Step 6 — Attach the same EBS again

In AWS Console:

EC2 → Elastic Block Store → Volumes

Select:

vol0ca4e0e03074422c2

Then:

Actions → Attach volume

Select your same EC2 instance.

For device name, AWS may show something like:

/dev/sdf

Wait until:

State = In-use

Step 7 — Find the disk in Linux

Now run:

lsblk -o NAME,SIZE,FSTYPE,MOUNTPOINTS

The EBS should appear again.

Don't assume it will always be /dev/nvme2n1.

After detach/reattach, Linux/AWS can give it a different NVMe device name.

=> /dev/disk/by-id/

Step 8 — Mount it again

Suppose it comes back as:

/dev/nvme2n1

Then:

sudo mount /dev/nvme2n1 /data-ebs

If it comes back as another device, for example:

/dev/nvme3n1

then use:

sudo mount /dev/nvme3n1 /data-ebs

Use whichever device actually belongs to vol0ca4e0e03074422c2.

then use:

sudo mount /dev/nvme3n1 /data-ebs

Use whichever device actually belongs to vol0ca4e0e03074422c2.
Click Attach volume.

